# Item-slot indicator rendering styles TODO:
    Objective:
        Support multiple presentations of the same indicator value and color.
        Freshness initially supports SlotBackground, SlotOutline, and HorizontalBar.
        Keep SlotBackground as the default.

    Style behavior:
        SlotBackground: existing bottom-up fill, optional draw ranges, and boundary cues.
        SlotOutline: complete thin border; provider color communicates freshness, not perimeter length.
        HorizontalBar: bottom-aligned track with left-to-right fill proportional to the actual resource fraction.
        Use scaled GUI units for outline thickness, bar height, and inset.
        Applicable outlines and bar tracks remain visible at zero; bar fill is absent at zero and full at one.
        Absent provider results and zero-opacity indicators remain hidden.

    1. Presentation contract:
        ☑ Add the rendering-style enum.
        ☑ Let registrations declare default/supported styles and an optional selector.
        ☑ Resolve styles independently for primary and overlay layers.
        ☑ Carry the resolved style in ItemSlotIndicatorRenderSelection.
        ☑ Fall back to the registration default for unsupported or invalid selections.

    2. Presentation selection:
        ☑ Keep provider applicability, fraction, color, draw range, and palette responsibilities unchanged.
        ☑ Resolve style independently of cached samples so configuration changes apply immediately.
        ☑ Ensure style switches do not resample freshness or trigger adaptive resource activity.

    3. Style layout:
        ☑ Add a focused layout component for background bounds, four non-overlapping outline strips, and bar track/fill bounds.
        ☑ Reuse the prepared rectangle mesh and engine GUI shader without per-slot resource allocation.
        ☑ Make draw-input validation retain applicable zero-valued outlines and bar tracks.

    4. Renderer integration:
        ☐ Dispatch ordinary drawing by style while preserving clipping, depth masking, and engine shader/state ownership.
        ☐ Preserve primary/overlay selection and render order, including independent food-amount particles.
        ☐ Apply draw ranges and boundary cues only to backgrounds; use unmapped fractions for bar length.
        ☐ Declare effect-supported styles, defaulting existing effects to SlotBackground.
        ☐ Draw the selected ordinary style when effects are incompatible, unavailable, or fail.
        ☐ Keep optional effect backgrounds compatible with the selected presentation.
        ☐ Switch styles without recreating prepared resources or shared simulations.

    5. Freshness configuration and documentation:
        ☐ Add PerishableItemFreshnessIndicatorStyle with SlotBackground as its default.
        ☐ Expose all three choices through ConfigLib with localized labels and descriptions.
        ☐ Configure freshness registration to support all three styles and select from configuration.
        ☐ Preserve other registrations' existing background behavior.
        ☐ Update indicator documentation and link this checklist from relevant project.todo entries.

    6. Validation:
        ☐ Test defaults, invalid/unsupported selections, immediate switching with cached samples, and overlay independence.
        ☐ Test zero/partial/full layout, GUI scaling, clipped positions, and outline corners without overlapping alpha blends.
        ☐ Test zero-value visibility, style-correct effect fallback, and background-only boundary cues.
        ☐ Run relevant project tests and normal build/package checks.
        ☐ Keep any temporary tests within the actual project test suite; do not launch standalone temporary test programs.
        ☐ Check all three freshness presentations in-game, including meal containers with food particles.

    Completion:
        ☐ Freshness switches styles through configuration, existing providers/effects retain their defaults, and all validation above passes.
        Note: automated checks do not establish in-game visual acceptance.
